.Dd August 22, 2026
.Dt XOCC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xocc
.Nd compiler wrapper for libxo's LLVM/clang plugins
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm xocc
.Cm full | validate | precompile
.Ar compiler-args ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr xo_plugins 7
describes
.Nm libxo Ns 's
two optional LLVM/clang plugins,
.Nm xo_validate
and
.Nm xo_precompile ,
and how to invoke them by hand with
.Fl fplugin=
and
.Fl fpass\-plugin= .
Wiring those flags into another project's build by hand means finding
the
.Pa .so
paths, the SDK
.Fl isysroot ,
and the
.Fl L Ns Ar dir
/
.Fl lxo
link flags, and repeating them on every compile and link line.
.Pp
.Nm
is a small shell wrapper, generated at configure time, that already
knows all of that.
It stands in for
.Ev CC
in another project's build: pass it wherever that project's build
system expects a compiler, and it runs the real compiler with
.Nm libxo Ns 's
plugin flags (and, at link time,
.Fl lxo )
already added.
.Pp
.Nm
is generated from
.Pa llvm\-plugins/xocc.sh.in
by
.Ic configure
(via
.Ic AC_CONFIG_FILES )
and installed as
.Pa ${bindir}/xocc
\(en it only exists, and only makes sense, in a
.Nm libxo
tree that was configured with the plugins enabled.
.Sh CONFIGURING
.Nm
is only useful if
.Nm libxo
itself was configured with
.Fl \-with\-llvm\-config ,
exactly as described in
.Xr xo_plugins 7 Ns 's
.Sx BUILDING THE PLUGINS
section:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&../configure \-\-with\-llvm\-config=/opt/local/libexec/llvm\-19/bin/llvm\-config \e
    \-\-prefix ~/work/root
.Ed
.Pp
The same
.Fl \-with\-sdk\-path=PATH
(or
.Fl \-with\-sdk\-path=no )
override applies here too;
.Nm
passes the configured SDK path to the real compiler as
.Fl isysroot
whenever it invokes the LLVM clang directly.
.Pp
If
.Fl \-with\-llvm\-config
was omitted, the plugins aren't built,
.Nm
still gets generated and installed, but it won't have real plugin
.Pa .so
files to point at.
Build and install
.Nm libxo
normally afterward:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cd build
make CFLAGS='\-O2'
make install
.Ed
.Pp
This installs
.Nm
into
.Pa ${prefix}/bin
alongside the rest of
.Nm libxo .
.Sh INVOKING XOCC
Unlike a normal
.Ev CC ,
.Nm
requires a verb as its first argument, telling it which plugin flags
to add:
.Bl -tag -width "validate"
.It Cm full
add both
.Nm xo_validate
and
.Nm xo_precompile .
.It Cm validate
add
.Nm xo_validate
only.
.It Cm precompile
add
.Nm xo_precompile
only.
.El
.Pp
Any other first argument (including a bare compiler flag or a
.Pa .c
file, which means the verb was left off) is an error, and
.Nm
refuses to run rather than silently compiling without
.Nm libxo Ns 's
flags.
.Pp
The verb is consumed before the rest of the command line is handed to
the real compiler, so
.Nm
can otherwise be dropped in wherever a compiler is expected.
The most common use is setting
.Ev CC
for a
.Ic make Ns \-based
build:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
make CC='xocc full'
.Ed
.Pp
The quotes matter \(en
.Ev CC
becomes the two-word command
.Ic xocc full ,
and
.Ic make
passes the rest of its usual compiler invocation
.Po
.Fl c Ar foo.c Fl o Ar foo.o ,
or the final link line
.Pc
after it.
To check format strings without paying for precompilation, or vice
versa, swap in the narrower verb:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
make CC='xocc validate'
make CC='xocc precompile'
.Ed
.Pp
.Nm
picks which real compiler to invoke, in order:
.Bl -enum
.It
.Ev XO_REAL_CC ,
if set \(en an explicit override, e.g. when the project's build
otherwise assumes a specific compiler:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
XO_REAL_CC=/usr/local/bin/clang\-19 make CC='xocc full'
.Ed
.It
the LLVM clang that built the plugins (the one found via
.Fl \-with\-llvm\-config
at
.Nm libxo Ns 's
own configure time), since the plugins are LLVM-version-specific and
only reliably load into a matching clang.
.It
otherwise,
.Nm libxo Ns 's
own build compiler, with the plugin flags added anyway if that
compiler isn't real
.Ic gcc
.Po Ic gcc
has no equivalent plugin support, so if it
.Em is
.Ic gcc ,
the plugin flags are dropped and the plain compiler is used
unmodified
.Pc .
.El
.Pp
.Nm
also inspects the rest of its arguments to tell a compile stage
.Po
.Fl c ,
.Fl E ,
or
.Fl S
present
.Pc
from a link stage.
At the link stage it adds
.Fl L Ns Ar libdir
.Fl lxo
automatically, so linking a program that used
.Nm
for its compiles needs no extra
.Nm libxo
flags:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xocc full \-c myprogram.c \-o myprogram.o
xocc full myprogram.o \-o myprogram
.Ed
.Pp
Both plugin flags, the SDK
.Fl isysroot ,
and the link flags come from values baked in when
.Nm libxo
itself was configured \(en
.Nm
needs no
.Fl I Ns Ar dir
/
.Fl L Ns Ar dir
flags pointing back at the
.Nm libxo
tree, beyond whatever the project's own build already passes for
.Nm libxo Ns 's
headers.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width XO_REAL_CC
.It Ev XO_REAL_CC
Overrides the real compiler
.Nm
invokes, taking priority over the LLVM clang detected at
.Nm libxo Ns 's
configure time.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libxo 3 ,
.Xr xo_emit 3 ,
.Xr xo_options 7 ,
.Xr xo_plugins 7
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
wrapper first appeared in
.Fx XX.y (unknown) .
